
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Client-only it is. I'll design the storage seam so a real backend can replace the source later without rewriting
  consumers.

  Next question: what "persist" should mean. This one is a lifetime decision, and lifetimes are a security property — a
  longer one means an unattended or shared machine keeps showing someone as logged in.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — per-tab, cleared when the tab closes. Safest default; the identity can't outlive the browsing
    session. Cost: a new tab starts logged out, which users often read as a bug.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restart, persists until explicitly cleared. Best "stays logged in" feel, shared
    across tabs. Cost: on a shared or unattended machine the identity sticks around indefinitely, and clearing it
    becomes a logout feature you now have to build.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module-level variable, gone on refresh. Not really persistence; I'm listing it only so we can
    rule it out against your "persist" requirement.

  Since this is a self-asserted username rather than an auth token, the blast radius of the longer lifetime is smaller
  than it would be with a real credential — but it still decides whether you need a logout path and cross-tab behavior,
  which are work either way.

  My recommendation is [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with an explicit clear/logout function designed in from the start, because it
  matches what you described ("persist, work across the app") and the logout path is cheap now and awkward to retrofit.
